--- /dev/null
+++ b/packages/fcl-tls/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,158 @@
+# Explicit smart TLS selection
+
+`fcl-tls` is a package above `fcl-net`, `openssl` and `gnutls`. It requires the
+native Schannel unit and the Network.framework/client-peer changes first.
+It adds no further HTTP/socket-core changes. The target is the FPC 3.3.1 branch;
+use a compiler and RTL built from that branch.
+
+```pascal
+uses fphttpclient, smartsslsockets, tlsbackendpolicy;
+var Client: TFPHTTPClient;
+begin
+  InitializeSmartTLS; // once, before loaders or clients; defaults to stbAuto
+  Writeln(SmartTLSBackendName(SmartTLSSelection.Backend));
+  Client := TFPHTTPClient.Create(nil);
+  try
+    Client.VerifySSLCertificate := True;
+    Writeln(Client.Get('https://example.com/'));
+  finally
+    Client.Free;
+  end;
+end.
+```
+
+The dotted units are `System.Net.SmartSSLSockets` and
+`System.Net.TLSBackendPolicy`. On Unix threaded applications put `cthreads`
+(`UnixApi.CThreads`) first, as usual.
+
+## Choice and failure policy
+
+Call `InitializeSmartTLS(stbOpenSSL)`, `stbGnuTLS`, `stbSchannel`, or
+`stbNetworkFramework` for an explicit backend. The optional second argument
+is an existing, absolute executable path; the default comes from the operating
+system, not `ParamStr(0)`. `SmartTLSSelection` reports the backend, canonical
+executable path, actual library paths, version, and diagnostic.
+`CreateSmartTLSHandler` creates the selected handler explicitly; ordinary
+`fphttpclient` uses its registered default after initialization.
+
+Auto tries complete bundled OpenSSL first, then bundled GnuTLS, then Windows
+Schannel or macOS Network.framework. Missing candidates permit the next step.
+A present incomplete pair, invalid image, wrong CPU, missing required client
+symbol, unsupported version, or mismatched SSL/crypto dependency fails explicitly.
+Initialization failure and successful selection are retained. Repeating the same
+request returns that result; changing the preference/path fails. Connection,
+certificate, callback, handshake and IO failures never select another backend.
+There is no verification downgrade. An explicit native override ignores bundled
+external libraries.
+
+Application roots are the executable directory and its `lib` subdirectory.
+For `Name.app/Contents/MacOS/...`, also search `Contents/Frameworks` and
+`Contents/lib`, between those two roots. Resolve symlinks/junctions; a library
+must remain within the application root (`Contents` for an app bundle).
+On older Windows without the final-path API, unresolved reparse paths fail;
+ordinary paths remain supported. No working-directory or PATH scan identifies
+bundled libraries. Native API availability follows the prerequisite backends.
+No Secure Transport or legacy macOS fallback is added.
+
+Within each root the supported filename profiles are tried in this order:
+
+| OS | OpenSSL pair | GnuTLS |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Windows | `libssl-3-x64.dll`/`libcrypto-3-x64.dll` (or `-arm64` on ARM64), `libssl-3.dll`/`libcrypto-3.dll`, `libssl-1_1-x64.dll`/`libcrypto-1_1-x64.dll` on x64, `libssl-1_1.dll`/`libcrypto-1_1.dll`, `libssl.dll`/`libcrypto.dll` | `libgnutls-30.dll`, `libgnutls.dll` |
+| macOS | `libssl.3.dylib`/`libcrypto.3.dylib`, `.1.1.dylib`, unversioned `.dylib` pair | `libgnutls.30.dylib`, `libgnutls.dylib` |
+| Linux | `libssl.so.3`/`libcrypto.so.3`, `.so.1.1`, unversioned `.so` pair | `libgnutls.so.30`, `libgnutls.so` |
+
+OpenSSL must be 1.1.1 or major 3; GnuTLS must be at least 3.6.0. The legacy
+Windows `ssleay32.dll`/`libeay32.dll` names are recognized after the profiles
+above, so an old bundle fails clearly rather than being silently ignored.
+Pairs are never assembled across roots/profiles. Dependencies of a bundled
+library still follow the operating system's loader rules; applications must
+package those dependencies correctly. This selector does not install or alter
+library search paths.
+
+## Loader compatibility and certificate options
+
+Existing explicit `opensslsockets` and `gnutlssockets` imports retain their
+behavior when the smart unit is absent. Importing smart requires the explicit
+initializer before using the default TLS handler. The smart initializer clears
+defaults registered by its dependencies, then registers the chosen concrete
+class only after selection succeeds. No lazy factory or core API is added.
+
+The new atomic `InitSSLInterfaceIfUnloaded` and `LoadGnuTLSIfUnloaded` APIs
+prevent selection from reusing or replacing an already initialized target
+loader. Even a matching earlier initialization fails: call smart first or use
+the existing explicit backend. The probe releases only its own module references.
+There is no global ownership claim, unload, replacement, reset, or automatic
+finalization. Existing loader entry points keep their normal semantics; GnuTLS
+loader operations now share a lock. Applications must not destroy/free/replace
+the selected loader while using smart handlers. New handler creation detects an
+explicitly freed interface and fails without reloading or changing selection.
+
+`TFPHTTPClient.VerifySSLCertificate` still defaults to False; smart preserves
+default, True, False and the handler's after-create setting. Set True for system
+trust/hostname validation with the native backends. External backends retain
+their own trust configuration; bundling OpenSSL does not give it the Windows
+system trust store. A small smart GnuTLS subclass installs automatic certificate
+verification only for True. The plain GnuTLS class keeps its existing behavior
+through a default virtual setup hook. GnuTLS >=3.6 uses its normal implicit
+library initialization; callers disabling that upstream mechanism must provide
+normal global initialization themselves.
+
+The selected concrete backend's capabilities still apply: the native providers
+are clients, reject Accept, and reject unsupported custom credentials/configuration.
+Network.framework owns TCP and uses the prerequisite client-peer hook. Its
+unsupported proxy/Unix socket/SSE/extraction settings fail explicitly. Native
+EOF behavior and HTTP framing are unchanged by smart. See the native backend
+documentation/tests for their limitations; smart is not a server abstraction.
+
+## Reproduce tests
+
+Build the compiler cycle, matching RTL and packages using the repository's
+normal guide. For a full package build include `packages/fpmake_add.inc` and
+`fpmake_proc.inc`; both register this package. For dotted packages build a dotted
+RTL separately and use `fpmake compile -ns --unitoutputdir=units/dotted
+-o '-dFPC_DOTTEDUNITS'` with its unit directory and RTL package metadata.
+
+```text
+python packages/fcl-tls/tests/buildtests.py --source SOURCE --compiler COMPILER --rtl RTL_UNITS --output TEST_OUT
+python packages/fcl-tls/tests/testsmartmatrix.py --source SOURCE --compiler COMPILER --rtl STANDARD_RTL_UNITS --program TEST_OUT/testsmartselection --output MATRIX_OUT
+```
+
+Use `.exe` on Windows. Add `--dotted` to `buildtests.py` and pass dotted RTL
+for that build. On macOS pass `--sdk SDK_PATH --minimum 10.8` (Intel) or `11.0`
+(ARM64); deployment metadata does not prove old-system runtime support.
+
+The matrix runs a fresh process per case, including 16 concurrent initializers.
+Its deliberately minimal fixture DLLs/dylibs are **selection-only**, never TLS
+implementations or evidence of real GnuTLS networking. Windows uses the existing
+Pascal compiler to build fixtures; Unix uses the existing C compiler for fixtures.
+Production code remains Pascal and uses the chosen TLS implementation.
+
+For real Windows no-third-party-library tests, leave `TEST_OUT` free of bundled
+DLLs and run the prerequisite's fixture against `testsmartnative.exe`:
+
+```text
+python packages/fcl-net/tests/schannel/testtls.py TEST_OUT/testsmartnative.exe EXISTING_OPENSSL_CLI FRESH_FIXTURE_OUT --online
+```
+
+This includes constructor/module audit, certificate status-specific rejections,
+fragmented 1 MiB receive/upload, HTTP framing, timeouts and close_notify handling.
+For real external HTTPS, copy actual application libraries/dependencies and
+`testsmarthttp` into a separate directory, then run:
+
+```text
+python packages/fcl-tls/tests/testsmartexternal.py --program APP/testsmarthttp --openssl EXISTING_OPENSSL_CLI --backend openssl --output FRESH_FIXTURE_OUT
+```
+
+Use `--backend gnutls` with real GnuTLS libraries. This matrix isolates trusted
+hostname/expiry failures, untrusted chains, True/False/default/after-create,
+callback veto and one-connection reuse; a timeout cannot pass certificate rejection.
+For actual macOS smart/native acceptance use the prerequisite's
+`testnetworktls.py`, with `testsmartnetworkhttp` as its HTTP program and the
+unchanged native/public-peer programs. All fixture certificates stay local;
+tests never change the OS trust store.
+
+Record OS/CPU, compiler/library versions, compile/package logs and every matrix
+result. Windows results do not establish macOS runtime behavior. A missing real
+GnuTLS runtime, untested architectures/OS versions, failed public network cases,
+or unrun checks must remain explicit in the review record.
